					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="4980602"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1549039343 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Feb 2019 16:42</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4138248">
									

<p>It's nice to see your writing character independent of co-authors (as nice as <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-4003" target="_blank">SCP-4003</a> is).</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Thoughts</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>You have a command of appropriate tone that is developed beyond your years, and you swap from a sharp clinical one to a more congenial, human one well and with good effect here. My favorite part of the article is the ability log, which dazzles with delightful physics concepts and is very clever.</p>
<p>I think you've achieved your goal of communicating the raw nitty-gritty of Foundation research; this offers a perspective usually taken for granted, and reminds us that any given science textbook's sentence can require a mountain of hard work (and sometimes suffering).</p>
<p>I was glad to see the text blinking effect justified in-article. But the bottom line about it from me is that it is very distracting. I had to highlight any given entry featuring it as well as any surrounding entry just to keep my eyes focused enough. Maybe that's a me thing. Another formatting nitpick would be that it seems strange that the the last note is center aligned.</p>
<p>The line where the researcher recognizes the wormhole strikes me as odd (per the article, they were up until this point theoretical, no?). Also, I feel as though the threat at the end is somewhat stuck on. I think the article would have been more effective with a bit more of a modest reach, ending at the researcher losing her arm (there's a pun in there somewhere).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Novote leaning towards upvote for now, but I will check back and see if some time or any changes push me in that direction. Good work imo.</p>

							</div>

								
					
